# TimeVault settings (template).
#
# Copy this file to ".env" next to timevault-server.exe (or into the folder you start it from),
# replace every <placeholder> and remove the lines you do not need. The service reads the file
# itself when it starts. A setting that is also present in the process environment wins over
# this file (the start-up log names every setting it overrides), so do not leave TIMEVAULT_*
# variables in the environment of the service. TIMEVAULT_ENV_FILE=<path> names another file;
# it must exist.
# Never commit a filled-in copy: it holds passwords. See docs/runbook.md.
#
# The monitored folders and their schedules are NOT set here. An administrator manages them on
# the Settings page of the web UI and they are stored in the database.

# --- Listener ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Loopback by default. Anyone who can reach this address can browse the backed-up files, and the
# user accounts' passwords are sent to it: put a TLS reverse proxy in front before other
# computers use it.
TIMEVAULT_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8660
# Only when listening on a non-loopback address that a TLS reverse proxy protects.
# TIMEVAULT_ALLOW_NON_LOOPBACK=true
# Set to true when the site is served over HTTPS.
TIMEVAULT_COOKIE_SECURE=false
# The path the site is served under when a reverse proxy gives it a path of a host name that other
# applications share, such as /tv for http://<host>/tv/. Leave it out when the site is at the root
# of its address. Every address the pages, redirects and cookies give out then carries the prefix,
# and a request with or without the prefix is accepted, so the proxy may keep it (recommended) or
# take it off. Letters, digits and - _ . ~ only; not a path of the application itself (api, static,
# login, explorer, ...). See "Behind a reverse proxy under a path" in docs/runbook.md.
# TIMEVAULT_BASE_PATH=/tv

# --- PostgreSQL -------------------------------------------------------------
TIMEVAULT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://timevault:<password>@127.0.0.1:5432/<database>
# true applies migrations at start; otherwise run "timevault-server migrate" first.
TIMEVAULT_AUTO_MIGRATE=false

# --- Storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup folder (the repository): where the contents of the backed-up files are stored. Keep it
# separate from every monitored folder; empty or already a TimeVault repository.
# Changing it needs a restart.
TIMEVAULT_REPOSITORY_PATH=<path-to-backup-folder>
# Restore folder: a restore never overwrites the original files. It writes the restored copies
# into a new folder below this one, and you copy back what you need. Empty disables restore.
# It must not be inside or contain the repository or a monitored folder.
TIMEVAULT_RESTORE_ROOT=<path-to-restore-folder>

# --- Network paths people see -----------------------------------------------
# People open the monitored folders and the restore folder over the network (SMB), so they know
# them as \\server\share\folder, not as the real folder on this server (D:\...). Only administrators
# see the real folders; everybody else sees these network paths, and texts that quote a real
# folder are rewritten.
# Name of this server as people type it in network paths. With it set, every monitored folder needs
# a display folder on this host (the Settings page checks it), because the search and the restore
# of this server only work with its own folders. Without it, display folders are optional.
# TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST=<server.example.local>
# The restore folder as people reach it over the network; shown next to restored copies.
# TIMEVAULT_RESTORE_DISPLAY_ROOT=\\<server.example.local>\<share>\<restore-folder>

# --- Sign-in ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Anyone can browse without signing in. Backing up, restoring, the audit log and the settings
# need an account.
#
# Company accounts are read from Redis (the app_user_* hashes). Check the connection with:
#   timevault-server check-redis-users
# redis://[:password]@host:port/db  (rediss:// for TLS). The account's Type "admin" may change
# the monitored folders; every other active account may back up and restore.
TIMEVAULT_REDIS_URL=redis://:<password>@127.0.0.1:6379/0
# TIMEVAULT_REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=app_user_
# TIMEVAULT_REDIS_TIMEOUT=3s
#
# Web log: lines about the pages that people open, the requests that failed and what people and the
# scheduler do (the audit events), appended to a list in this same Redis (the same database), the
# web log that the company applications share. auto (the default) is on when TIMEVAULT_REDIS_URL is
# set; on needs TIMEVAULT_REDIS_URL; off writes nothing. The Redis account has to be allowed to RPUSH
# to the list, otherwise the service logs a warning and drops the lines. TimeVault never trims the
# list. See "Web log" in docs/runbook.md.
# TIMEVAULT_WEB_LOG=auto
# The list. ASCII letters, digits and _ . : - only; it must not start like TIMEVAULT_REDIS_KEY_PREFIX
# (above) or sso_login_, the keys of the accounts and of the single sign-on results.
# TIMEVAULT_WEB_LOG_KEY=web_log
# The last field of every line. Not set: the name of this computer in lower case.
# TIMEVAULT_WEB_LOG_HOST=<host-name>
#
# Optional local administrator who can still sign in when Redis is down. Leave both lines out for none.
# TIMEVAULT_ADMIN_USER=admin
# Output of: timevault-server hash-password
# TIMEVAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=<hash-from-hash-password>
#
# Base64 text that decodes to at least 32 random bytes; keeps sessions across restarts.
TIMEVAULT_SESSION_KEY=<base64-of-32-or-more-random-bytes>
TIMEVAULT_SESSION_TTL=8h

# Single sign-on with the Windows account (optional). The login page then offers a second button
# that opens the company sign-on page (the same one the dash application uses) in a window; the
# sign-on service records who the browser is in Redis (sso_login_<token>, the same Redis as above)
# and TimeVault signs that employee in, if the account in Redis is active. Leave empty for none.
# The address may change, so it is a setting: host, host:port or http(s)://host[:port].
# TIMEVAULT_SSO_HOST=<sso.example.local>
# The sign-on service records the address of the browser it saw. With this on (the default), the
# sign-in only succeeds when the browser that asked for it has that address, so nobody can sign in
# as a colleague by getting the colleague to open a sign-on link. Needs the real address of the
# browser: behind a reverse proxy set TIMEVAULT_TRUSTED_PROXIES; only if that is impossible set
# this to false.
# TIMEVAULT_SSO_REQUIRE_SAME_IP=true
# Reverse proxies (addresses or CIDR ranges, comma separated) whose X-Forwarded-For header is
# believed. The proxy must put the address of the browser it saw into that header (in nginx:
# proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr; never $http_x_forwarded_for in the first proxy).
# Without this setting TimeVault only knows the address of the connection, which is the proxy's:
# the audit log and the sign-in throttle then see every user as the proxy.
# With another proxy in front of that one, see docs/runbook.md, "Two proxies in a row".
# TIMEVAULT_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1,::1
#
# Pictures of the accounts, shown next to a name in the header, the audit log, the job pages and the
# permission lists. The browser reads each picture from this address, with {id} for the employee ID.
# Not set: http://<TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST>/api/static/hr/{id}.webp, where the other company
# applications keep the pictures of the employees (no pictures while TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST is not
# set). A full http(s) address reads them from another host (the content security policy of the site
# is extended to it; a host name with an underscore is covered by its domain). A path such as
# /api/static/hr/{id}.webp reads them from the host the site is opened from. An account without a
# picture shows the default picture. off turns the pictures off.
# TIMEVAULT_AVATAR_URL=http://<server.example.local>/api/static/hr/{id}.webp

# --- Search (optional) ------------------------------------------------------
# Everything HTTP server on this computer. Leave empty to turn search off. It also gives the
# Explorer the size of every folder (turn on Tools > Options > Indexes > "Index folder size" in
# Everything) and can tell the scheduler that a folder did not change (see below).
# TIMEVAULT_EVERYTHING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:<port>/
# TIMEVAULT_EVERYTHING_USERNAME=<username>
# TIMEVAULT_EVERYTHING_PASSWORD=<password>
# TIMEVAULT_EVERYTHING_TIMEOUT=5s

# --- Scheduler (automatic backups) ------------------------------------------
# Automatic backups run one at a time and spread out, so the server does not feel slow. The
# schedules of the folders are set on the Settings page; the settings below only pace them.
# How many automatic backups may run at the same time. Backups started by hand are not limited.
# TIMEVAULT_SCHEDULER_PARALLEL=1
# Least time between the starts of two automatic backups. auto = what keeps up with all the
# schedules (10 folders with an interval of 60 minutes: one start every 6 minutes), within 30
# seconds and 10 minutes. A duration such as 5m sets it; 0s turns the spacing off.
# TIMEVAULT_SCHEDULER_GAP=auto
# Pause after an automatic backup before the next one starts (only while PARALLEL is 1). auto =
# as long as the backup took, within 30 seconds and 10 minutes, so a big folder is followed by a
# longer pause. A duration such as 1m sets it; 0s turns it off.
# TIMEVAULT_SCHEDULER_REST=auto
# Ask Everything whether a folder changed before scanning it, and skip the scan when nothing was
# created, modified, renamed, moved or deleted below it. It needs TIMEVAULT_EVERYTHING_URL, an
# Everything that monitors the volume of the folder and "Index recently changed date" on. A
# folder is scanned anyway when Everything does not answer or does not know it, when its last
# scan left items uncaptured, and at least every FULL_SCAN_EVERY.
# TIMEVAULT_SCHEDULER_EVERYTHING_PROBE=false
# TIMEVAULT_SCHEDULER_FULL_SCAN_EVERY=24h
# Priority of the service among the other programs of this computer: normal, below_normal (the
# default) or background (also lowers the disk priority). A low priority keeps a backup scan from
# slowing down the file shares of the same server.
# TIMEVAULT_PROCESS_PRIORITY=below_normal

# --- Automatic updates (optional) -------------------------------------------
# The service can replace itself with a newer build. The build step (scripts\release.ps1) writes a
# release folder with timevault.exe and timevault.json (latest_version, sha256, package_url, size);
# the service reads that manifest and, when latest_version is not the version that runs, downloads
# the program, checks it, stops taking new jobs, waits for the jobs that run to finish, puts the new
# program in place of the old one and starts it. The old program is kept next to it
# (timevault.old-<version>.exe) for a manual way back.
# The address of the manifest, or of the folder that holds it: an http(s) address or the absolute
# path of a folder or a network share. With the static files of the other company applications it is
# http://<TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST>/api/static/cd/timevault.json: copy timevault.exe, then timevault.json,
# to the static\cd folder of that application. A package_url in the manifest that is only a file name
# is looked for next to the manifest (http://<TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST>/api/static/cd/timevault.exe).
# Whoever can change that folder decides which program runs here, so only administrators may write to
# it. The address is always written here: it is not derived from TIMEVAULT_SERVER_HOST. Needs
# TIMEVAULT_AUTO_MIGRATE=true. Leave empty for no automatic updates.
# TIMEVAULT_UPDATE_URL=http://<server.example.local>/api/static/cd/timevault.json
# How often the manifest is read (1m to 24h). The Settings page has a "Check now" button.
# TIMEVAULT_UPDATE_INTERVAL=10m
# How the new program is started. supervise (the default): the process you started stays as a small
# launcher and starts the new program, which works from a console window and from a scheduled task.
# exit: the process ends with the code 75 and whatever started it starts it again: a service manager
# (NSSM), or a scheduled task whose trigger repeats every minute with "Do not start a new instance"
# (the task setting "If the task fails, restart" does not restart a program that ended with 75).
# TIMEVAULT_UPDATE_RESTART=supervise

# --- Performance and logging ------------------------------------------------
TIMEVAULT_JOB_WORKERS=2
# TIMEVAULT_HASH_WORKERS=4
TIMEVAULT_LOG_LEVEL=info
TIMEVAULT_LOG_FORMAT=text
